# XSH readv()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# readv() is an XSI function. It behaves as read() except where the page
# says otherwise, and its return value and errors are read()'s (module
# xsh/read tests those for read()). Error tests arrange exactly one error
# condition (XSH 2.3).

assertion  XSH.readv.001
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/readv.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() on a regular file with three buffers of 2, 3 and 4
           bytes and 9 bytes available returns 9, with the data in
           file order across the buffers, each filled before the next.
test       t/001-gather.c

assertion  XSH.readv.002
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() on a regular file holding 5 bytes, with buffers of 3,
           4 and 4 bytes, returns 5: the first buffer holds the first 3
           bytes and the second starts with the last 2.
test       t/002-short.c

assertion  XSH.readv.003
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() advances the file offset by the number of bytes read,
           so a second readv() continues where the first stopped.
test       t/003-offset.c

assertion  XSH.readv.004
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A zero-length entry among the buffers takes no data: with
           lengths 2, 0, 3, readv() returns 5 and the data fills the
           first and third buffers in order.
test       t/004-zero-entry.c

assertion  XSH.readv.005
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() on a regular file at its end returns 0 and leaves the
           buffers alone.
test       t/005-eof.c

assertion  XSH.readv.006
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A successful readv() that reads data marks the file's last data
           access time for update.
note       Takes about 2 s, as XSH.read.014.
timeout    20
test       t/006-atime.c

assertion  XSH.readv.007
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() on a pipe that holds 4 bytes, with buffers of 3 and 3,
           returns at once with between 1 and 4 bytes, which are the
           pipe's first bytes in order across the buffers.
test       t/007-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.readv.008
anchor     functions/readv.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/read.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() fails with EBADF on a descriptor that is not open, and
           on one open only for writing.
test       t/008-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.readv.009
anchor     functions/readv.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/read.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() on an empty pipe that has a writer, with O_NONBLOCK
           set, fails with EAGAIN.
test       t/009-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.readv.010
anchor     functions/readv.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() with two buffers whose lengths sum to more than the
           largest ssize_t value (each is SSIZE_MAX, on a small file)
           fails with EINVAL and reads nothing.
test       t/010-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.readv.011
anchor     functions/readv.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() with iovcnt 0, or greater than {IOV_MAX}, either fails
           with EINVAL or is carried out; no other error is allowed (the
           call may fail, and nothing is said of what happens when it
           does not). When {IOV_MAX} cannot be found the second call is
           skipped.
note       The result is NOTINUSE when the system carries both calls out.
test       t/011-iovcnt.c

assertion  XSH.readv.012
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       readv() with iovcnt 1 reads like read(): the buffer is filled
           from the file offset and the offset advances.
test       t/012-single.c

assertion  XSH.readv.013
anchor     functions/readv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       readv() with iovcnt equal to {IOV_MAX} (one-byte entries over a
           regular file of that many bytes) is valid: it succeeds and
           reads all {IOV_MAX} bytes, in order.
note       NOTINUSE when {IOV_MAX} cannot be found or is too large to
           allocate here.
test       t/013-iov-max.c
